# Sales Commission Plan — FY26: quota relief

> Module QuotaRelief

> Using Commission

This module computes the Quota against which Attainment is measured, after
relief for qualifying absence under S-6. The *data* flows relief-first: S-6.2
says relief is applied before Attainment is calculated, so the relieved Quota is
an input to `Commission.Attainment` and never a correction applied to a figure
computed from the assigned Quota.

The *module* dependency runs the other way, which is deliberate. S-6.1 has to
know the Plan Year, and S-1.1 — which is the only place the Plan Year window is
stated — is encoded in `Commission`; restating the window here would be a second
copy of a clause that exists once. `CommissionAfterRelief` below then composes
the whole chain in the order S-6.2 fixes. Nothing in `Commission` imports this
module, so there is no cycle.

Nothing here is a judgement input. The three absence categories S-6.1 names are
legal statuses payroll records, and the 30-day continuous-period threshold is
what supplies the content of "long-term" — the Plan does not leave that to an
assessor.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration enumeration AbsenceCategory:
  -- StatutoryParentalLeave
  -- AdoptionLeave
  -- LongTermSicknessAbsence
  -- OtherAbsence

# One continuous absence, inclusive of both endpoints: an absence recorded as
# starting and ending on the same day is one day of absence.
declaration structure AbsencePeriod:
  data category content AbsenceCategory
  data start_date content date
  data end_date content date

declaration scope QualifyingAbsence:
  input absence content AbsencePeriod
  output days_of_absence content integer
  output is_qualifying_category content boolean
  output is_qualifying content boolean
  output in_year_days_of_absence content integer

declaration scope RelievedQuota:
  input annual_quota content money
  input absence_periods content list of AbsencePeriod
  internal qualifying_category_absences content list of AbsencePeriod
  internal continuous_absence_windows content list of date
  output relief_days content integer
  output relief_exact content decimal
  output relief content money
  output relieved_quota_exact content decimal
  output relieved_quota content money
  output quota_for_attainment_exact content decimal
  output quota_for_attainment content money

declaration scope CommissionAfterRelief:
  input assigned_quota content money
  input absence_periods content list of AbsencePeriod
  input net_booked_revenue content money
  input new_logo_share content decimal
  input commencement_date content date
  input annual_base_salary content money
  output quota_relief scope RelievedQuota
  output commission scope Commission.CommissionPayable
  output quota_for_attainment content money
  output commission_payable content money
```

## S-6 Quota relief — the gateway

| COMM-PLAN S-6.1 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:83)
|
| Where an Employee is absent on statutory parental leave, adoption leave
| or long-term sickness absence for a continuous period of 30 days or more, the
| Employee's Quota is reduced by 1/365th of the annual Quota for each day of such
| absence.

S-6.1 asks two questions and the whole of this module's correctness depends on
keeping them apart. The **gateway** question is whether the Employee "is absent
... for a continuous period of 30 days or more". Two unconnected 20-day
absences do not combine, and "30 days or more" is inclusive, so a 30-day
absence qualifies and a 29-day one does not. The **quantum** question — by how
much the Quota falls — is answered separately below.

Days are counted inclusively of both endpoints, which is the ordinary reading
of an absence "from" one date "to" another: 1 January to 30 January is 30 days.
`days_of_absence` is the length of the whole continuous absence, wherever its
days fall, because that is what the gateway is measured on.

This scope answers the gateway for **one record taken alone**, which is the
answer where the record is the whole absence. It is not where the gateway is
finally decided: the clause asks about a continuous period of absence, and one
continuous absence can be written down as several abutting records, so
`RelievedQuota` below asks the question of the absence rather than of the
paperwork (CE-0028). `is_qualifying` remains reportable because the two-question
structure of the clause is what the rest of the module turns on, and because a
record that passes the gateway on its own length may still relieve no day of
the Plan Year (CE-0012, CE-0013). It tests the same threshold as the continuity
section below, `continuous_period_length`, which is declared there with the
clause it comes from: the clause states "30 days or more" once, so the module
holds it once and both readings of the gateway consume it.

```catala
scope QualifyingAbsence:
  definition days_of_absence equals
    integer of ((absence.end_date - absence.start_date) / (1 day)) + 1

  definition is_qualifying_category equals
    absence.category with pattern StatutoryParentalLeave
    or absence.category with pattern AdoptionLeave
    or absence.category with pattern LongTermSicknessAbsence

  definition is_qualifying equals
    is_qualifying_category
    and days_of_absence >= continuous_period_length
```

## S-6.1 Which days can be relieved — the Plan Year confines them

| COMM-PLAN S-6.1 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:83)
|
| Where an Employee is absent on statutory parental leave, adoption leave
| or long-term sickness absence for a continuous period of 30 days or more, the
| Employee's Quota is reduced by 1/365th of the annual Quota for each day of such
| absence.

The days S-6.1 can relieve are days of the Plan Year, and only those. S-1.1
confines the whole Plan to the financial year 1 October 2025 to 30 September
2026; S-2.1 makes the Quota the annual target assigned for that Plan Year; and
S-6.1's unit of relief — 1/365th of the annual Quota — is a proration of that
same 365-day year, there being no other 365-day period in the instrument for the
fraction to refer to. A day that is not a day of the Plan Year therefore has no
1/365th of this Quota to consume. An absence lying wholly in another financial
year relieves nothing (CE-0012), and an absence straddling the boundary passes
the gateway on its full continuous length while relieving only its in-year days
(CE-0013) — the two questions of the clause, answered separately.

`in_year_days_of_absence` is this record's days clipped to the Plan Year window,
which is imported from `Commission` rather than restated. It is not itself a
quantum of relief: overlapping records would each count the shared days, which
is what S-6.1 forbids and what `RelievedQuota` below is careful not to do.

```catala
scope QualifyingAbsence:
  definition in_year_days_of_absence equals
    let first_day equals
      Date.max of absence.start_date, Commission.plan_year_start
    in
    let last_day equals
      Date.min of absence.end_date, Commission.plan_year_end
    in
    if last_day <= first_day then 0
    else integer of ((last_day - first_day) / (1 day)) + 1
```

## S-6.1 The gateway is a fact about the absence, not about the records

| COMM-PLAN S-6.1 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:83)
|
| Where an Employee is absent on statutory parental leave, adoption leave
| or long-term sickness absence for a continuous period of 30 days or more, the
| Employee's Quota is reduced by 1/365th of the annual Quota for each day of such
| absence.

**CE-0028.** One 40-day sickness absence certified by two successive fit notes
reaches this module as two abutting records — 1 to 20 January and 21 January to
9 February — with no day of attendance between them. Tested per record, each is
20 days, neither passes the 30-day gateway, and the Employee gets nothing; the
identical absence written as a single record relieves 40/365ths. S-6.1 conditions
relief on the Employee being "absent ... for a continuous period of 30 days or
more", which is a fact about how long she was absent and not about how many
entries the HR system holds, so an answer that turns on the filing is wrong. The
records must therefore be combined into continuous absences **before** the
gateway is applied.

Combining them needs none of the machinery this module refused when it came to
the quantum — no sort, no adjacency rule, no clipping rule — because the
continuity question is local to a day. A day belongs to a continuous period of
absence of 30 days or more exactly when some run of 30 consecutive days, every
one of them a day of qualifying absence, contains it. So the module tests runs:
`continuous_absence_windows` holds each day from which the next 30 days are all
covered by some record, and a day of the Plan Year is relievable when it lies in
one of those windows. Abutting records merge under that test because coverage is
taken over the records **together** (1 to 20 January and 21 January to 9
February cover 1 January to 9 February with no gap), overlapping and duplicate
records merge for the same reason, and a single day of attendance between two
20-day records still breaks the period, because no 30-day run is then fully
covered. The 30-day figure is the clause's own and is named once,
`continuous_period_length`, so the gateway and the window length cannot drift
apart.

The windows are assessed over a span wider than the Plan Year, because the
gateway is measured on the whole continuous absence wherever its days fall
(CE-0013): a window containing a day of the Plan Year can start up to 29 days
before the Plan Year does, and none can start after its last day.

Only the categories S-6.1 names can contribute a covered day. The clause
relieves absence "on statutory parental leave, adoption leave or long-term
sickness absence" and its list is disjunctive, so a 20-day parental leave
abutting a 20-day sickness absence is one continuous period of qualifying
absence and passes the gateway; whereas a spell of some other absence between
two sickness records is a day on which the Employee was absent on no ground
S-6.1 names, and so breaks the period rather than bridging it.

```catala
# Was the Employee absent on this day, on one of the grounds S-6.1 names,
# according to some record? This predicate is taken over the records together,
# which is what merges abutting and overlapping ones.
declaration day_of_qualifying_absence content boolean
  depends on d content date, periods content list of AbsencePeriod
  equals
    exists p among periods such that p.start_date <= d and d <= p.end_date

# S-6.1's own threshold: "a continuous period of 30 days or more".
declaration continuous_period_length content integer equals 30

declaration window_is_continuous_absence content boolean
  depends on w content date, periods content list of AbsencePeriod
  equals
    for all i among (List.sequence of 0, continuous_period_length) we have
      day_of_qualifying_absence of (w + i * (1 day)), periods

# A window that contains a day of the Plan Year starts no earlier than 29 days
# before the Plan Year and no later than its last day, so those are the
# candidates. The span is derived from S-1.1's window and from the threshold
# above rather than written out.
declaration continuity_window_starts content list of date equals
  let days_in_plan_year equals
    integer of
      ((Commission.plan_year_end - Commission.plan_year_start) / (1 day)) + 1
  in
  let n equals days_in_plan_year + continuous_period_length - 1 in
  map each i among (List.sequence of 0, n)
  to Commission.plan_year_start
     - (continuous_period_length - 1) * (1 day)
     + i * (1 day)

scope RelievedQuota:
  definition qualifying_category_absences equals
    list of a among absence_periods
    such that
      (output of QualifyingAbsence with { -- absence: a }).is_qualifying_category

  definition continuous_absence_windows equals
    list of w among continuity_window_starts
    such that window_is_continuous_absence of w, qualifying_category_absences
```

## S-6.1 The quantum — one day, one 1/365th

| COMM-PLAN S-6.1 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:83)
|
| Where an Employee is absent on statutory parental leave, adoption leave
| or long-term sickness absence for a continuous period of 30 days or more, the
| Employee's Quota is reduced by 1/365th of the annual Quota for each day of such
| absence.

The unit of account in the quantum limb is the **day of absence**, not the
absence record. A day on which the Employee was absent is one day of absence
however many leave categories the HR system records against it, so it attracts
one 1/365th reduction and no more. Summing record lengths double-counts the days
two overlapping records share (CE-0014) and, worse, lets relief exceed 365/365ths
and drive the Quota negative — an outcome no reduction "by 1/365th ... for each
day" of a 365-day year can ever produce.

`relief_days` is therefore the number of days **of the Plan Year** that fall
within a continuous period of qualifying absence of 30 days or more, as the
section above determines. Catala has no set type, and the two ways to take a
union of date ranges are to merge the ranges or to count the days; this module
counts the days, for three reasons. It is the clause's own unit: "for each day of
such absence" is a predicate over days, and `plan_year_days` is exactly the
domain the 1/365th fraction prorates. It needs no machinery the clause does not
mention — no sort, and no rule for clipping one range against another. And it
makes both invariants structural rather than argued: a day is counted once
because it is one day, and the count cannot exceed the days of the window.

That is also what keeps CE-0014's property and CE-0028's fix from pulling
against each other. The gateway above merges abutting and overlapping records
into continuous absences, but it never adds a day to the count: the count runs
over the days of the Plan Year, asking of each whether it lies in some
continuous absence, so two records that share 47 days contribute those 47 days
once however many records mention them, and two abutting records contribute
their 40 distinct days exactly once as well.

```catala
# Every day of the Plan Year, from S-1.1's window, which is inclusive at both
# ends and so spans (end - start) + 1 days. `List.sequence` stops one short of
# its second argument, so the day count is itself that argument. Deriving the
# list from the window keeps it from drifting from S-1.1.
declaration plan_year_days content list of date equals
  let days_in_plan_year equals
    integer of
      ((Commission.plan_year_end - Commission.plan_year_start) / (1 day)) + 1
  in
  map each i among (List.sequence of 0, days_in_plan_year)
  to Commission.plan_year_start + i * (1 day)

scope RelievedQuota:
  definition relief_days equals
    number of (list of d among plan_year_days
               such that (exists w among continuous_absence_windows
                          such that w <= d
                                and d <= w
                                     + (continuous_period_length - 1)
                                       * (1 day)))
```

## S-6.1 The reduction, and the ceiling the clause implies

| COMM-PLAN S-6.1 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:83)
|
| Where an Employee is absent on statutory parental leave, adoption leave
| or long-term sickness absence for a continuous period of 30 days or more, the
| Employee's Quota is reduced by 1/365th of the annual Quota for each day of such
| absence.

The reduction itself is the arithmetic the clause states: 1/365th of the annual
Quota per relieved day. The assertions record what the day-counting above makes
true — the window is the 365-day period the 1/365th unit presupposes, and no day
can be counted twice — so relief can never exceed the annual Quota and the
relieved Quota can never go negative, an outcome the clause cannot produce.

**CE-0027.** The reduction is carried as an exact `decimal` of pounds, and the
`money` figures beside it are reports of it rather than steps in it. S-6.1
reduces the Quota "by 1/365th of the annual Quota for each day of such absence"
and specifies no rounding of that reduction; the Plan confers no power to round
it either. Yet 30 days on a £750,000 Quota is £61,643.8356164383..., which no
`money` value can hold, because Catala fixes `money` at the cent. Rounding it
there — to £61,643.84, four ten-thousandths of a penny too much — shrank the
relieved Quota to £688,356.16 and lifted Attainment from 49.99999968% to exactly
50%, crossing the S-4.1(a)/(b) boundary and moving £27,534.25. So the chain that
S-6.2 feeds to Attainment is exact throughout (`relief_exact`,
`relieved_quota_exact`, `quota_for_attainment_exact`), and the conversion to
`money` happens only where the Plan produces a sum of money: at S-3.1's product,
in `Commission`. This is the same defect as CE-0015 and CE-0023, and the same
remedy.

`relief` and `relieved_quota` are kept as `money` because they are what a review
letter or a payslip states, and they are the figures the earlier
counterexamples pin (CE-0012 to CE-0014). They are rounded to the nearest penny
from the exact quantities and are read by nothing: no arithmetic in this module
or in `Commission` consumes them, so the rounding cannot propagate into an
answer. The assertion on the exact relieved Quota, not on the rounded one, is
the invariant that matters.

```catala
scope RelievedQuota:
  definition relief_exact equals
    (decimal of annual_quota) * (decimal of relief_days / 365.0)

  definition relieved_quota_exact equals
    (decimal of annual_quota) - relief_exact

  definition relief equals money of relief_exact

  definition relieved_quota equals money of relieved_quota_exact

  assertion number of plan_year_days = 365
  assertion relief_days <= 365
  assertion relieved_quota_exact >= 0.0
```

## S-6.2 Relief is applied before Attainment is calculated

| COMM-PLAN S-6.2 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:88)
|
| Quota relief under S-6.1 is applied before Attainment is calculated.

S-6.2 is an ordering rule with a concrete consequence: the denominator of the
S-2.2 quotient is the relieved Quota, not the assigned Quota. That consequence
is carried across the module boundary by naming the quantity —
`quota_for_attainment_exact` is what `Commission.Attainment`'s `quota` input is
fed from, in the exact form CE-0027 requires, with `quota_for_attainment` the
same quantity rounded for reporting — and a caller that passed `annual_quota`
instead would be computing a
different and lower Attainment, a different accelerator under S-4 and, because
S-5.2 puts the cap last, a different capped figure too. The identity is the
whole of the clause: S-6.2 adds no arithmetic of its own, it fixes which
arithmetic comes first.

```catala
scope RelievedQuota:
  definition quota_for_attainment_exact equals relieved_quota_exact

  definition quota_for_attainment equals relieved_quota
```

| COMM-PLAN S-6.2 (003-sales-commission-plan.md:88)
|
| Quota relief under S-6.1 is applied before Attainment is calculated.

Naming the quantity is not enough, and the adversarial reviewer was right that
it was all this module did. `Commission.CommissionPayable` takes `quota` as a
free input, so until now whether S-6.2 was honoured depended on the caller
remembering to pass `quota_for_attainment` rather than the assigned figure — and
no probe could tell the two apart, because both are just a `money` input. On an
assigned Quota of $1,000,000 with 73 qualifying absence days and Net Booked
Revenue of $800,000 the difference is the whole of an S-4.1 band: 100% Attainment
and an accelerator of 1.5 giving $96,000, against 80% and 1.0 giving $64,000.

**CE-0026.** A composition is also where two modules can disagree about what is
possible, and this one did. S-6.1 reduces the Quota to nil where a qualifying
absence covers the whole Plan Year — 365 days, each consuming its 1/365th — so
`RelievedQuota`'s output domain includes £0 on facts the Plan compels; but
`Commission.Attainment` carried `assertion quota > $0` and its quotient divided
by that nil Quota, so the composition raised a division by zero on a question
the Plan answers plainly. S-3.1 fixes commission at £0.00 where Net Booked
Revenue is £0, whatever the accelerator.

The seam was closed on the `Commission` side, because that is the side that was
wrong. A nil relieved Quota is lawful — S-6.1 produces it and S-6.2 requires
Attainment to be taken on it — so a guard refusing it refused an input the
documents produce, which is a defect in the guard and not in the input. What the
old guard was really aimed at, a quota-bearing Employee with no assigned Quota
at all, survives there in narrower form; the reasoning is set out under S-2.2 in
`Commission`. Nothing needed to change here: this scope already passed on what
S-6.1 and S-6.2 compel.

`CommissionAfterRelief` closes that gap structurally. It takes the *assigned*
Quota and the absence records, and there is no path through it that reaches
`Commission.CommissionPayable` without passing through `RelievedQuota` first:
the sub-scope's `quota` input is defined here, once, as the exact relieved
figure (CE-0027), and
`absence_periods` is a required input that cannot be defaulted away — an
Employee with no absence is stated as the empty list, affirmatively, rather than
by omission. The ordering S-6.2 mandates is therefore a fact about the code
rather than a convention in the prose. Nothing else is re-encoded: S-2.2, S-3.1,
S-4 and S-5 keep their single encoding in `Commission`, and this scope only
wires them up in the mandated order.

```catala
scope CommissionAfterRelief:
  definition quota_relief.annual_quota equals assigned_quota
  definition quota_relief.absence_periods equals absence_periods

  definition quota_for_attainment equals quota_relief.quota_for_attainment

  definition commission.quota equals quota_relief.quota_for_attainment_exact
  definition commission.net_booked_revenue equals net_booked_revenue
  definition commission.new_logo_share equals new_logo_share
  definition commission.commencement_date equals commencement_date
  definition commission.annual_base_salary equals annual_base_salary

  definition commission_payable equals commission.commission_payable
```
